 "name": "Forest Scenic Beauty and Recreation Satisfaction (rating)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Paudyal, Stein, Ober, Swisher, Jokela & Adams (2018), Forests 9:241. Participants list, in order of importance, the three main activities they do on forest visits. They then see 12 photographs of Ocala National Forest (Florida, USA). The forest there is managed with prescribed burning and cutting as habitat for the endangered red-cockaded woodpecker. For each photo they rate scenic beauty (1 = not at all scenic … 10 = very scenic) and expected recreation satisfaction (1 = not at all satisfied … 10 = very satisfied). In the paper, an expert panel later marked six pictures as good or poor woodpecker habitat. These are tagged in the data so habitat index scores can be compared across activity groups. In the original study (209 on-site visitors), the mixed pine–hardwood stand scored highest (8.2) and a recently cut stand lowest (3.1).\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) online instead of a laminated photobook shown to visitors on site. (2) Pictures are shown in the order of Supplementary Figure S1, one fixed order for everyone; the paper used one fixed random order, which it does not list. (3) Pictures come from the supplement figure at about 470 px width, so they are smaller than the 10.5 × 7.25 inch prints. (4) Activities are typed as free text; the original also asked an open list. (5) The demographic answer options (gender, education, employment) were written for this template. Race, ethnicity and income are left out.\n\nPhotographs: Paudyal et al. (2018), Figure S1 (CC BY 4.0). Used with credit for research; the template is removed if the authors object.",
